  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nMerry-go-round high pressure will control our weather over the \nnext 10 days. An area of upper level high pressure is currently \ncentered over Colorado and this upper level ridge will make a slow\ntour of the southwest this week and by the start of next week, \nwill end up pretty much back where it started over Colorado.\n\nFor Monday, like I said, the ridge will centered over Colorado \nwhich gives us an easterly flow aloft. Embedded in this flow has \nbeen a very slow moving weak upper level trough. This trough will \nbe centered more or less over northern Mexico, Monday afternoon. \nThis trough will help spark wide spread thunderstorm activity and \nwith precipitable water values (PW's) of 1.3 to 1.4 inches across \nthe region, storms will be very efficient rain makers. The \ngreatest threat for heavy rain will be in Luna, Hidalgo and \nsouthern Grant Counties where a Flash Flood Watch is currently in \neffect. These locations have already see good amounts of rain on \ntonight and last night and it looks like they will be under the \ngun again Monday afternoon. With all the clouds and moisture, our \nhigh temperatures will be running several degrees below average. \nMany lowland locations may not make it out of the 80's for high \ntemperatures.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, the upper level ridge will drift into \nthe Red River Vally of Oklahoma and Texas. This ridge position \nwill begin to push the moisture further and further to the west. \nFor Tuesday there will be enough moisture to give everyone a \nchance for rain, but by Wednesday much of the moisture will be \npushed into Arizona, so the best rain chances will be along the \nNew Mexico and Arizona border. High temperatures both days will \ncontinue to run a few degrees below average. \n\nFor the end of the week, into the weekend, the upper level ridge \nwill build directly over New Mexico and that will pretty much shut\ndown our rain chances and it will jump most lowland location's \nhigh temperatures back above the triple digit mark. For the start \nof the final week in July the ridge will return to Colorado. It \ndoesn't look like much moisture will be able to slip into the \nregion from the east so the hot dry weather looks to continue as \nwe finish out July.",
